phase1.code:
import java.util.*;

public class IteratorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(
            Arrays.asList("Apple", "Banana", "Cherry", "Date", "Elderberry")
        );

        System.out.println("Original: " + list);

        // 1. For-each loop (read-only)
        System.out.println("=== For-Each Loop ===");
        for (String fruit : list) {
            System.out.println("  " + fruit);
        }

        // 2. Iterator
        System.out.println("=== Iterator ===");
        Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            String fruit = it.next();
            System.out.println("  " + fruit);
        }

        // 3. Removing while iterating with Iterator
        System.out.println("=== Removing with Iterator ===");
        it = list.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            String fruit = it.next();
            if (fruit.startsWith("A") || fruit.startsWith("D")) {
                it.remove();  // Safe removal!
            }
        }
        System.out.println("After removing A and D: " + list);

        // 4. ListIterator (bidirectional)
        System.out.println("=== ListIterator (forward) ===");
        ListIterator<String> listIt = list.listIterator();
        while (listIt.hasNext()) {
            String fruit = listIt.next();
            System.out.println("  " + fruit);
        }

        System.out.println("=== ListIterator (backward) ===");
        while (listIt.hasPrevious()) {
            String fruit = listIt.previous();
            System.out.println("  " + fruit);
        }

        // 5. ListIterator with modification
        System.out.println("=== ListIterator with Add/Set ===");
        listIt = list.listIterator();
        while (listIt.hasNext()) {
            String fruit = listIt.next();
            if (fruit.equals("Banana")) {
                listIt.set("Blueberry");  // Replace
            }
            if (fruit.equals("Cherry")) {
                listIt.add("Cranberry");  // Insert after
            }
        }
        System.out.println("After modifications: " + list);

        // 6. For-each with map
        System.out.println("=== For-Each with Map ===");
        HashMap<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();
        scores.put("Alice", 95);
        scores.put("Bob", 87);
        scores.put("Charlie", 92);

        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : scores.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println("  " + entry.getKey() + " = " + entry.getValue());
        }

        // 7. Iterator with primitive array
        System.out.println("=== Array with for-each ===");
        int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        for (int num : numbers) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        // 8. Performance comparison
        System.out.println("=== Iterator vs For-Each ===");
        // For-each is syntactic sugar for iterator
        // Both have same performance
        // For-each is simpler, but iterator gives more control

        // 9. ConcurrentModificationException demo
        System.out.println("=== Preventing ConcurrentModification ===");
        List<String> list2 = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C", "D"));

        // BAD: Remove during for-each
        // for (String s : list2) {
        //     if (s.equals("B")) {
        //         list2.remove(s);  // ConcurrentModificationException!
        //     }
        // }

        // GOOD: Use iterator
        Iterator<String> it2 = list2.iterator();
        while (it2.hasNext()) {
            if (it2.next().equals("B")) {
                it2.remove();  // Safe!
            }
        }
        System.out.println("After safe removal: " + list2);

        // Alternative: removeIf
        list2.removeIf(s -> s.equals("C"));
        System.out.println("After removeIf: " + list2);
    }
}

phase1.explanation:
The for-each loop you've used since Unit 1 — for (String s : list) — has never been magic. [[key:every for-each loop over a collection is syntactic sugar for grabbing an Iterator and calling hasNext()/next() in a loop]] — the compiler quietly writes that boilerplate for you, every single time, like an assistant who's already memorized the same set of steps.

Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    String s = it.next();
    System.out.println(s);
}

So why reach for Iterator directly instead of just writing for-each every time? [[warn:one specific reason: safe removal]]. You already saw back in the ArrayList lesson that mutating a list mid-for-each throws ConcurrentModificationException — for-each is a read-only tour of the shelf, it has no mechanism to remove anything mid-walk. it.remove() is different: it's the one operation allowed to modify the collection while iterating, precisely because the iterator is the thing keeping track of exactly where you're standing.

[[term:ListIterator]] extends this further, only on Lists: it can walk backward with hasPrevious()/previous(), and it can add() or set() elements mid-traversal, not just remove them — none of which a plain Iterator or a for-each loop can do.

[[fun:the practical rule of thumb]]: reach for plain for-each when you're only reading the shelf. Reach for Iterator the moment you need to remove elements while walking it. Reach for ListIterator on the rare occasion you need to insert, replace, or walk backward mid-loop. Or skip the manual loop for simple filtering entirely — list.removeIf(condition) does the same safe-removal job in one line.

phase2.brokenCode:
import java.util.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C"));

        // Forgot to call next() before remove()
        Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            it.remove();  // ERROR!
        }
    }
}

phase2.explanation:
[[warn:IllegalStateException]] from Iterator.remove() — you can't call remove() twice in a row, or before next() has been called at all.

it.remove() doesn't take an argument — [[key:it removes whatever element the most recent next() call just returned]]. Call it a second time without calling next() again first, and there's nothing new for it to remove — the iterator has no "current element" anymore, so it throws rather than silently doing nothing or removing the wrong thing.

The fix is exactly one next() per remove():

while (it.hasNext()) {
    String s = it.next();
    if (shouldRemove(s)) {
        it.remove();  // exactly one remove() per next()
    }
}

This pairing is the whole contract of Iterator.remove() — think of it as "remove the thing I just looked at," never "remove something."

phase2.fixedCode:
import java.util.*;

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of("A", "B", "C"));
        Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            String s = it.next();
            if (s.equals("B")) {
                it.remove();  // exactly one remove() per next()
            }
        }
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}

